{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/821988-the-tone-of-owod-vs-chronicles-of-darkness","id":821988,"title":"The Tone of OWoD vs Chronicles of Darkness","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-02-02T17:14:28-08:00","id":821988,"title":"The Tone of OWoD vs Chronicles of Darkness","content":"Im a HUGE fan of the Old Wod, but i always felt it to be more Horror Action prone (a la Aliens if ya know what i mean). i didnt knew where to bring the disussion but...according to you (other than the metaplot) whats the main differences between both version? which is Darker? or more Slowpaced? which is more mysterious and more creepier?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Forum's Official's Joker and Trickster. Pardon my bad english, aint my first language (I Speak Spanish).<br /> ST: DtF, HtR, WtO, MtA<br /> Signature Chars: Crowley (hakalu), Joe The Nuwisha (WtA)<br /> Changelings: be afraid of the Technocracian High Five of Doom","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1754-crowley","name":"Crowley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1754_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-02T17:27:54-08:00","id":822001,"title":"","content":"The biggest difference, IMO, is the meaning of the word Darkness.<br /> <br /> In World of Darkness the darkness tends to be malice. The world is not merely harsh, it is deliberately cruel. The Man is out to get you, there are monsters that go bump in the night, and they want to bump <b>you</b>. The Darkness isn't something that can be avoided, or even denied. The world is awful, the apocalypse is coming, and there is nothing you can do about it. But you fight none the less, because it's even worse if you don't.<br /> <br /> In Chronicles of Darkness the darkness is mystery. There is a terrible, alien, unknowable world out there. But for the most part it's <b>out there</b>. There's a distinct vibe of everyone just trying to get by, vampires don't control the world, Werewolves aren't fighting for <i>your </i>survival. What they face is strange and awful, but also very personal. Personal is a very important word in CofD, everything is personal. Stories, even reality spanning tales, tend towards the personal. DaveB actually made an excellent post outlining this difference between Ascension and Awakening:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's a bit at the end of Ascension (the novel, not the game supplement, that finished M:TAsc's metaplot) in which the world ends during the time of judgement and everyone and everything is reduced to it's component spheres. All the mages Ascend, become aware of the full span of the lives of all human beings. The Ascension war stops, and the last thing they all do - Technocrats, Orphans and Traditions alike - is heartfealtly congratulate one another for their efforts to better humanity. It was hopeful, optimistic - it felt appropriate as an ending to a game where everyone is trying to better the lot of humanity, but are fighting over how to do it.<br /> <br /> Wouldn't happen in Awakening, beyond the obvious reasons. Because Mages in Awakening? They're bastards.<br /> <br /> The path to ascension in Awakening (and it's odd that the game that isn't named after the concept has the clearer view of what it means, and seems - to me at least - as being a game in which it could happen more realistically) is a deeply personal one. Mages in Ascension lose their individual hangups as they progress, realising their paradigms don't matter. Mages in Awakening *gain* something akin to a paradigm as their soul evolves along a Legacy. It's the other way around.<br /> <br /> Because the path is so personal, because they have empirical proof that it's themselves they need to worry about and not anyone else, because they're used to living under the shadow of jealously guarding who they are from friends and co-workers for fear of someone getting an advantage... Awakening Mages are an awful lot more narcissistic. Selfish fucks were the bad apples of Ascension, but they're the operating standard of Awakening - everyone is out for number one. </div> </div> </div>And that I really think sums it up. WoD is about a terrible world, and striving to correct it. CofD is about terrible people, and striving not to be one of them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":19}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-02T20:32:41-08:00","id":822090,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PenDragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/821988-the-tone-of-owod-vs-chronicles-of-darkness?p=822001#post822001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The biggest difference, IMO, is the meaning of the word Darkness.<br /> <br /> In World of Darkness the darkness tends to be malice. The world is not merely harsh, it is deliberately cruel. The Man is out to get you, there are monsters that go bump in the night, and they want to bump <b>you</b>. The Darkness isn't something that can be avoided, or even denied. The world is awful, the apocalypse is coming, and there is nothing you can do about it. But you fight none the less, because it's even worse if you don't.<br /> <br /> In Chronicles of Darkness the darkness is mystery. There is a terrible, alien, unknowable world out there. But for the most part it's <b>out there</b>. There's a distinct vibe of everyone just trying to get by, vampires don't control the world, Werewolves aren't fighting for <i>your </i>survival. What they face is strange and awful, but also very personal. Personal is a very important word in CofD, everything is personal. Stories, even reality spanning tales, tend towards the personal. DaveB actually made an excellent post outlining this difference between Ascension and Awakening:<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> And that I really think sums it up. WoD is about a terrible world, and striving to correct it. CofD is about terrible people, and striving not to be one of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like this a lot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-12T10:01:48-08:00","id":828843,"title":"","content":"It is one of the greater differences, indeed. And a very ample one.<br /> <br /> As example, CoD is local in a way WoD never really achieved to be (but was close at the very first edition of Vampire). CoD can be played for a wider scope, but it brights most at the local. The answer for how things are isn't important. How they impact your life, your neighborhood, your city at the very most. This is relevant.<br /> <br /> There are other distinctions, and the nature of Darkness is as <b>PenDragon</b> said. The Darkness of the WoD is about a mean world, a place of evil and corruption. It is rooted in the punk part of its themes. A nihilistic and critic view of the world. WoD is the world at its worst. CoD not so much. It is the darkness of the unknown. The mystery. Is not knowing what's really going on, and not being able to ever really know.<br /> <br /> Horror, in the WoD, is about the unavoidability of the fall of everything. The world will fall, your people will fall, you will fall. A true hero can rise and resist this, but it is unlikely to happen, and in the end, almost certainly meaningless. The horror of CoD is uncertainty, is victimization to something you can't control. In this regard, it inverts the relationship with the self. The horror from CoD, a more personal game, comes from without. And even when it comes from within, it haves a twist: it <u>isn't</u> unavoidable. It <u>isn't</u> impossible to detect or repent. So, it is that much more your fault when it comes. You have none to blame but yourself.<br /> <br /> Going for the specific questions (not that I think them to be comprehensive, as presented): None is darker, but each is dark in its own way. CoD tends to be more slowpaced, but it is also more versatile in this regard, so it will depend a lot on the group. CoD is more mysterious by far, though it also needs more input from the ST to construct the mysteries for the chronicle. Both can be creepy, but by its take on mystery, CoD also wins in general creepiness. That, though, is just a facet of both games.<br /> <br /> What you can do best is to try CoD (since you already plays WoD). I personally play and enjoy both games, and can't think of one as taking the place of the other.<br /> <br /> Oh, all that is letting rules aside. For the rules, you'll probably enjoy CoD much more by the way you talk. There are reasons to stick to WoD's rules when playing WoD, but in general CoD have far superior ones.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-04T12:21:41-08:00","id":842067,"title":"","content":"If I was to try CoD for vampire do I only need the Vampire the Requiem 2nd edition or do I need the CoD? The rules are the only thing that intrigue me. I actually like the WoD story and darkness. But the rules are always mentioned as better in Requiem. Can I run Requiem rules, but use WoD story, clans, sects very easily or will it require lots of conversions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11414-kilcannon","name":"Kilcannon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-04T12:52:52-08:00","id":842091,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kilcannon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/821988-the-tone-of-owod-vs-chronicles-of-darkness?p=842067#post842067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I was to try CoD for vampire do I only need the Vampire the Requiem 2nd edition or do I need the CoD? The rules are the only thing that intrigue me. I actually like the WoD story and darkness. But the rules are always mentioned as better in Requiem. Can I run Requiem rules, but use WoD story, clans, sects very easily or will it require lots of conversions?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd pick up Requiem 2nd ed, it contains most of the core rules, and you can see how they approach disciplines differently and interact with the CoD rules more fully. Generally speaking I prefer how they structured the Requiem disciplines over Masquerade.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4270-literatzi","name":"literatzi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4270_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-04T12:56:46-08:00","id":842096,"title":"","content":"But is it easy to convert to WoD for purposes of keeping the clans and sects and not changing the background of the world but just the rules?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11414-kilcannon","name":"Kilcannon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-10T11:32:04-08:00","id":845191,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kilcannon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/821988-the-tone-of-owod-vs-chronicles-of-darkness?p=842096#post842096\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But is it easy to convert to WoD for purposes of keeping the clans and sects and not changing the background of the world but just the rules?</div> </div> </div> </div>It depends. You've got most of your core disciplines; Dominate, Majesty (Presence basically), Obsfucate, Potence, Celerity, Fortitude, Protean, and Animalism. So the bulk of the Camarilla can easily be replicated. Clan weaknesses are easy enough to do in most cases, and you could probably tie them into the core rules pretty well.<br /> <br /> Malkavians might be a little harder, they moved away from derangements as a thing, but there are ways to replicate them. Dementation isn't a core discipline, but I wouldn't be surprised if they hadn't written it up as a bloodline thing. Tremere are in a similar boat, Requiem approaches blood magic in a very different way, but I think you can more easily port over Thaumaturgy paths and rituals.<br /> <br /> If you're going outside of the Camarilla there isn't as much support in the core. In that case you can either troll through the million different bloodlines in Requiem, or get the conversion guide. The only downside here is none of those were written for Requiem 2E, so there might be a little bit of work needed still. Or, you know, work out rules conversions for Obtenebration and things yourself.<br /> <br /> There are some rules you'll need to adjust. Blood Potency and Generation don't quite map 1:1, if I remember correctly in Requiem the ability to spend multiple blood per turn comes a bit easier, and in Masquerade that jump is at 9th Gen. So just pick what works best for you, and then just have Blood Potency be static. You'll find little things like that scattered around, but it should be easy enough to just defer to Masquerade when you find something like that.<br /> <br /> Humanity works in a similar manner, and they give some mechanical hooks to encourage you to detail your mortal ties.<br /> <br /> Requiem kind of condensed Masquerade and then took that farther. You'll see lots that is familiar, but handled slightly differently, as well as a ton of new ideas. I'd recommend giving it a look, maybe try and create a masquerade character using the system to see how easy it feels to you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4270-literatzi","name":"literatzi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4270_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-10T16:02:11-08:00","id":845336,"title":"","content":"Yeah just don't wanna spend the money on the books unless going to it. Got every Dark Ages boo created and probably 70 to 75% Masquerade","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11414-kilcannon","name":"Kilcannon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-25T03:38:21-07:00","id":852878,"title":"","content":"I've done a lot of work on conversion notes for running World of Darkness with CofD rules. A lot of cases, don't try to make square pegs fit and you'll do fine. Remove Generation as an actual mechanic; if you start off higher Generation, you simply spend your Merits for higher Blood Potency. Just use BP as is, Disciplines as is. For werewolves, remove the automatic &quot;heal all lethal/bashing each turn in Gauru,&quot; and Rage, allow them to take Gauru indefinitely as per usual, etc. Just run with the fact that aggravated damage is more rare. It's not really important to the concepts. A lot of the template abilities work fine as is, requiring a fluff change at most. The bulk of the work making it feel like WoD is in presentation and setting, really. Convert races or powers on a case-by-case basis, or use something that exists in the CofD if at all possible. If you have to convert something, try to look for similar powers extant in the rules, and don't get too hung up on replicating the exact mechanics.<br /> <br /> I want to run a World of Darkness game in the near future, but I will definitely do it with the Chronicles rules. Standardization and symmetry, plus generally faster and more flexible gameplay make it a win for me. My only real troubles in conversion are things heavily tied to concepts. Like, Ascension seems the hardest to run in Awakening to me; how do I reattach the former's fluff to the latter's rules? Everything else is pretty easy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}